    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface unit. The above commands show the equivalent configuration for all three interfaces mentioned in the Cisco configuration (ge-1/0/0, ge-1/0/1, and ge-1/0/2). Each interface needs to have IPv6 explicitly enabled in Junos, unlike Cisco's single `ipv6 enable` command per interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface unit. The above commands show the equivalent configuration for all three interfaces mentioned in the Cisco configuration (ge-1/0/0, ge-1/0/1, and ge-1/0/2). Each interface needs to have IPv6 explicitly enabled in Junos, unlike Cisco's single `ipv6 enable` command per interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface unit. The above commands show the equivalent configuration for all three interfaces mentioned in the Cisco configuration (ge-1/0/0, ge-1/0/1, and ge-1/0/2). Each interface needs to have IPv6 explicitly enabled in Junos, unlike Cisco's single `ipv6 enable` command per interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:3::2/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:3::2/64\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:DB8:3::2/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 pim [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 pim sparse-mode` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse\\n##\\n\\nNote: In the actual Juniper configuration, you would replace `<interface-name>` with the specific interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0.0`). The command needs to be applied to each interface where PIM sparse mode is required, similar to the Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces <interface-name> family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces <interface-name> family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you would replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0`). The `ipv6 enable` command in Cisco simply enables IPv6 processing on an interface, which in Juniper is achieved by configuring the `inet6` address family on the interface.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces <interface-name> family inet6'}]]": 1,
