    "['ipv6 pim [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 pim sparse-mode` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse\\n##\\n\\nNote: You would need to replace `<interface-name>` with the actual Juniper interface name (e.g., `ge-0/0/0`) when applying this command to specific interfaces.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols pim interface <interface-name> family inet6 mode sparse'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ipv6 interface all', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols ipv6 interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you need interface-specific IPv6 enabling (more precise equivalent to Cisco\\'s per-interface \"ipv6 enable\"), it would be:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces <interface-name> unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, IPv6 is typically enabled by configuring the \\'inet6\\' family on an interface, rather than having a separate enable command like Cisco. The first example enables IPv6 globally on all interfaces, while the second shows the interface-specific approach. You would replace \"<interface-name>\" with the actual interface name (e.g., \"ge-1/0/0\").', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ipv6 interface all'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces <interface-name> unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols ipv6 interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you need interface-specific IPv6 enabling (more precise equivalent to Cisco\\'s per-interface \"ipv6 enable\"), it would be:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces <interface-name> unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, IPv6 is typically enabled by configuring the \\'inet6\\' family on an interface, rather than having a separate enable command like Cisco. The first example enables IPv6 globally on all interfaces, while the second shows the interface-specific approach. You would replace \"<interface-name>\" with the actual interface name (e.g., \"ge-1/0/0\").', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces <interface-name> unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
